      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Image build/sign/release pipeline does not restrict the ref to main's ancestry, letting any pushed tag or dispatched branch become the repo-signed :1 image.\n\nThe untrusted source is the ref that triggers publish.yml (any pushed v* tag or any workflow_dispatch branch); the dangerous sinks it reaches without an authorization check are the registry push (line 62), the keyless cosign signing (line 71), and the GitHub Release (line 88). Unlike publish-ios-channel.yml (which gates on `git merge-base --is-ancestor \"$TAG_SHA\" origin/main`, line 57), publish.yml never verifies the built commit is on the protected main branch.\n\nImpact: A commit that was never merged to the protected `main` branch is published to GHCR as `:1`, `:<version>`, and `:sha-<sha>`, cosign-signed with the repository's keyless OIDC identity, and cut as a GitHub Release. The signature satisfies the exact `cosign verify ...:1 --certificate-identity-regexp '^https://github.com/AG-Studio-Apps/stackguard/' ...` command the README instructs users to run, so anyone pulling `:1` directly (per README) or auditing by cosign identity accepts attacker code that has full engine-socket (host) access on deploy. This bypasses the documented release-authorization control ('main is protected; releases are tagged from main after merge').\n\nExploit scenario: A collaborator with write-but-not-release-manager access (or a leaked CI token) pushes a tag `v9.0.0` pointing at a feature branch containing a backdoored agent, or dispatches publish.yml on that branch. publish.yml checks out and builds that commit, pushes it as `ghcr.io/ag-studio-apps/stackguard:9`/`:9.0.0`, and runs `cosign sign` over its digest with the repo's OIDC identity. A user following the README then pulls and cosign-verifies the tag, sees a valid repository signature, and runs the backdoored image against a Docker/Podman socket.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker holds repository write (push) access, or controls a CI credential/action with contents+id-token+packages scope on this repo (fork PRs cannot reach this: no pull_request trigger and forks get no id-token).; No tag-protection ruleset covers `v*` (GitHub's default), so `main`'s branch protection does not extend to tags — a `v*` tag can point at any commit.; For the workflow_dispatch vector, the actor selects an arbitrary branch to run on; checkout+`context: .` builds that branch's HEAD while `git describe` supplies the major tag.\n\nRecommendation: Before build/push/sign, add the same authorization gate publish-ios-channel.yml uses, e.g. `git merge-base --is-ancestor \"$GITHUB_SHA\" origin/main || { echo '::error::ref is not on main'; exit 1; }`, and reject a manual dispatch whose HEAD is not on main's ancestry. Additionally configure a GitHub tag-protection ruleset for `v*` and/or a protected `release` deployment environment so only authorized principals can run the signing job.\n\nCWE-862. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": ".github/workflows/publish.yml",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 18,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "    tags: [\"v*\"]"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "on.push trigger (publish job, .github/workflows/publish.yml)",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "on.push trigger (publish job, .github/workflows/publish.yml)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F1",
              "title": "Image build/sign/release pipeline does not restrict the ref to main's ancestry, letting any pushed tag or dispatched branch become the repo-signed :1 image",
              "impact": "A commit that was never merged to the protected `main` branch is published to GHCR as `:1`, `:<version>`, and `:sha-<sha>`, cosign-signed with the repository's keyless OIDC identity, and cut as a GitHub Release. The signature satisfies the exact `cosign verify ...:1 --certificate-identity-regexp '^https://github.com/AG-Studio-Apps/stackguard/' ...` command the README instructs users to run, so anyone pulling `:1` directly (per README) or auditing by cosign identity accepts attacker code that has full engine-socket (host) access on deploy. This bypasses the documented release-authorization control ('main is protected; releases are tagged from main after merge').",
              "file": ".github/workflows/publish.yml",
              "line": 18,
              "description": "The untrusted source is the ref that triggers publish.yml (any pushed v* tag or any workflow_dispatch branch); the dangerous sinks it reaches without an authorization check are the registry push (line 62), the keyless cosign signing (line 71), and the GitHub Release (line 88). Unlike publish-ios-channel.yml (which gates on `git merge-base --is-ancestor \"$TAG_SHA\" origin/main`, line 57), publish.yml never verifies the built commit is on the protected main branch.",
              "exploit_scenario": "A collaborator with write-but-not-release-manager access (or a leaked CI token) pushes a tag `v9.0.0` pointing at a feature branch containing a backdoored agent, or dispatches publish.yml on that branch. publish.yml checks out and builds that commit, pushes it as `ghcr.io/ag-studio-apps/stackguard:9`/`:9.0.0`, and runs `cosign sign` over its digest with the repo's OIDC identity. A user following the README then pulls and cosign-verifies the tag, sees a valid repository signature, and runs the backdoored image against a Docker/Podman socket.",
              "preconditions": [
                "Attacker holds repository write (push) access, or controls a CI credential/action with contents+id-token+packages scope on this repo (fork PRs cannot reach this: no pull_request trigger and forks get no id-token).",
                "No tag-protection ruleset covers `v*` (GitHub's default), so `main`'s branch protection does not extend to tags — a `v*` tag can point at any commit.",
                "For the workflow_dispatch vector, the actor selects an arbitrary branch to run on; checkout+`context: .` builds that branch's HEAD while `git describe` supplies the major tag."
              ],
              "category": "Missing Authorization",
              "severity": "MEDIUM",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "recommendation": "Before build/push/sign, add the same authorization gate publish-ios-channel.yml uses, e.g. `git merge-base --is-ancestor \"$GITHUB_SHA\" origin/main || { echo '::error::ref is not on main'; exit 1; }`, and reject a manual dispatch whose HEAD is not on main's ancestry. Additionally configure a GitHub tag-protection ruleset for `v*` and/or a protected `release` deployment environment so only authorized principals can run the signing job.",
              "cwe_id": "CWE-862",
